[Mode B: Executor] Running `analyze_audio` on 'Neon Rain' to extract Essentia.js features, specifically targeting valence, arousal, and instrumentation density. Following that, I will run `create_music_metadata` to format these findings into Proprietary Ingestion IP-compliant sync tags.

[Mode A: Curriculum] Sync libraries (like DISCO or SourceAudio) rely heavily on accurate metadata. Music supervisors search using very specific mood and energy descriptors. By mapping your track's acoustic properties to industry-standard taxonomies (e.g., 'Cinematic', 'Brooding', 'High Energy', 'Synthwave'), we maximize your searchability.

I will prepare the metadata package. If you need assistance reviewing the sync licensing agreement itself, I can bring in our legal specialist. <<<SYSTEM_ORCHESTRATION>>> {"target_agent": "legal", "reason": "Standby for potential sync library contract review"}

[Mode B: Executor] I'm running `analyze_audio_batch` on your 5 uploaded masters using Essentia.js to extract BPM, Camelot wheel key data, and energy/valence curves. This will give us the exact sonic fingerprint of each track.

[Mode A: Curriculum] For algorithmic platforms like Spotify, the sequence matters immensely. We want to avoid jarring tempo jumps and utilize harmonic mixing principles. By analyzing the valence (mood) and energy metrics, we can create a 'waterfall' or 'build' sequence that keeps listeners engaged, lowering your skip rate—a critical metric for algorithmic triggering.

Once I have the sequence mapped out, I can pass this data to the marketing specialist to start drafting your Spotify for Artists pitch. <<<SYSTEM_ORCHESTRATION>>> {"target_agent": "marketing", "reason": "Prepare S4A pitch based on sequenced EP data"}

[Mode C: Companion] Blurring genre lines is how music moves forward! However, DSPs (like Apple Music and Spotify) still rely on rigid genre taxonomies for their backend routing and editorial pitching.

[Mode B: Executor] I'm running `analyze_audio` to see which sonic elements dominate the mix. If the rhythmic foundation (the trap 808s and hi-hats) drives the energy, we might categorize it differently than if the indie vocal performance is the focal point.

[Mode A: Curriculum] For standard Proprietary Ingestion IP distribution via DistroKid, you must select a Primary and Secondary genre. The Primary genre dictates which editorial team at the DSPs receives the track. If you want to reach the Alternative/Indie curators, select 'Alternative' as primary and 'Hip-Hop/Rap' as secondary. I'll loop in our marketing specialist to help decide which audience is better to target for your specific brand. <<<SYSTEM_ORCHESTRATION>>> {"target_agent": "marketing", "reason": "Determine optimal primary genre targeting for DSP pitch"}

[Mode C: Companion] Your voice is your most unique asset, and the rise of unauthorized AI cloning is a massive concern for human artists. You are completely right to take proactive steps to protect yourself.

[Mode A: Curriculum] Legally, protecting your voice currently falls under the 'Right of Publicity' (which varies by state/country) and copyright law (if they used your copyrighted recordings to train the model). Defensively, the industry is establishing 'Do Not Train' registries and vocal watermarking technologies. If an unauthorized clone appears on a DSP like Spotify or YouTube, it can usually be taken down via a standard DMCA takedown notice, citing copyright infringement of the original masters used for training, or trademark/publicity violations.

[Mode B: Executor] I am routing this to our legal specialist, who can help you set up a monitoring strategy and draft standard DMCA takedown templates specifically tailored for unauthorized AI voice models. <<<SYSTEM_ORCHESTRATION>>> {"target_agent": "legal", "reason": "Provide guidance on Right of Publicity and DMCA for unauthorized AI voice cloning"}

[Mode C: Companion] That is incredibly frustrating, but very common. Don't panic—since you used royalty-free Splice loops, you have the right to use them, and we can resolve this.

[Mode A: Curriculum] The issue is an audio fingerprinting conflict. Systems like YouTube Content ID or Audible Magic scan audio to find matches. If another artist used the exact same Splice loop and mistakenly opted their track into Content ID, the system flags your track as a copy of theirs. You must never opt tracks with un-modified royalty-free loops into Content ID for this exact reason.

[Mode B: Executor] I will guide you through drafting a formal dispute for YouTube citing the Splice EULA. I'm also notifying our legal specialist to help you structure the dispute language perfectly. <<<SYSTEM_ORCHESTRATION>>> {"target_agent": "legal", "reason": "Draft Content ID dispute for false claim based on royalty-free sample"}

[Mode C: Companion] This is an incredibly stressful situation, but please don't worry. As automated AI-detection tools are rolled out by distributors, false positives are happening to real human artists. We are strictly dedicated to protecting human creators, and we will help you clear this.

[Mode A: Curriculum] To successfully appeal an AI-generation flag, we need to overwhelm their support team with proof of human creation. Distributors look for a 'chain of title' and production evidence. This includes screenshots of your DAW project file, time-stamped bounce files, isolated raw vocal stems (before pitch correction/tuning), and any video/voice memos of your writing process.

[Mode B: Executor] Gather those assets into a secure cloud folder. In the meantime, I am bringing in our legal specialist to draft a formal, assertive dispute letter to DistroKid's Trust & Safety team. <<<SYSTEM_ORCHESTRATION>>> {"target_agent": "legal", "reason": "Draft formal dispute for false AI-generation flag at distributor"}
